mixin ValidatableControlShellComponent-Abstract

  p.Article-Paragraph.
    Input elements like a single-line text field (#[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID ValidatableControlShell(type="text")]),
      a multi-line text input (#[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID textarea]), a dropdown list
      (#[+SecondaryEntity__Code--YDID select]) and so on are usually not used alone, but surrounded by
      auxiliary elements:

  ul.Article-UnorderedList
    li Caption (usually a #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID label] element)
    li Comment (for example, "dashes and brackets are optional" for a phone number input field)
    li "Required" or "optional" labels
    li Validation messages

  p.Article-Paragraph.
    According to one of the principles of quality design, #[+Keyword--YDID all] these surrounding elements should be
      made in a #[+Keyword--YDID single] style, so there is a need for a #[+Keyword--YDID template]
      #[+Term--YDID component] whose content will surround the input element.
    In #[+ImportantEntity--YDID YDF], such a component is #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID ValidatableControlShell] —
      literally, "the shell of a validatable component".
    As the name implies, this component #[+Keyword--YDID is not] independent, but
      #[+Keyphrase--YDID must be part of other components], or more precisely, be a #[+Keyword--YDID shell] for them.

  ul.Article-UnorderedList

    li
      | #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID ValidatableControlShell] itself #[+Keyword--YDID is not] a
      |
      +Link--YDF({
        internalURI: localizedRouting.coreLibrary.$children.GUI_Components.$children.controls.$children.validatables.$URI,
      }) validatable component
      | , because it only displays the validation messages passed to it, but at the same time it
      |   #[+Keyword--YDID does not] validate #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID anything] itself, since validation is the task
      |   of the main component that #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID ValidatableControlShell] surrounds.

    li.
      When choosing the name, the word "wrapper" was avoided as it is much more polysemantic than "shell".
      Thus, in relation to user interface components, "wrapper" can mean not only literally
        wrapping the main component on both sides with its elements, but also extending the functionality of the main
        component.


